    {
      "name": "exp1_title",
      "value": "Basic Elements of Morse Code"
    },
    {
      "name": "exp1_content",
      "value": "• Morse code is based on \"dots (·) and dashes (-)\"\n• Different combinations of dots and dashes represent letters, numbers, and symbols\n• This is the foundation of all Morse code and must be mastered"
    },
    {
      "name": "exp2_title",
      "value": "SOS Signal"
    },
    {
      "name": "exp2_content",
      "value": "• SOS is an emergency distress signal that needs quick recognition\n• Compact transmission: The three characters (··· --- ···) are sent compactly without normal character spacing\n• Standard spacing rules: Normally, Morse code characters should have 3-unit spacing between them\n• SOS exception: But SOS, as an emergency signal, ignores this rule and sends compactly for faster recognition"
    },
    {
      "name": "exp3_title",
      "value": "Importance of Timing Rules"
    },
    {
      "name": "exp3_content",
      "value": "• The 1:3:7 ratio is the core rule of Morse code\n• Dot = 1 unit, Dash = 3 units, Character spacing = 3 units, Word spacing = 7 units\n• This precise timing ensures accuracy and readability of information transmission"
    },
    {
      "name": "exp4_title",
      "value": "Historical Background"
    },
    {
      "name": "exp4_content",
      "value": "• Samuel Morse and Alfred Vail collaborated in the 1830-1840s\n• Initially used for telegraph systems, later evolved into international Morse code\n• Became the global standard for radio communication encoding"
    },
    {
      "name": "exp5_title",
      "value": "Learning Strategy"
    },
    {
      "name": "exp5_content",
      "value": "• Letters → Numbers → Symbols is the most scientific learning order\n• Master basic letters first, then learn numbers and symbols\n• Progressive learning to avoid information overload and improve efficiency"
    },
    {
      "name": "cexp1_title",
      "value": "Definition of Chinese Morse Code"
    },
    {
      "name": "cexp1_content",
      "value": "• Chinese Morse code is a coding system specifically designed for Chinese characters\n• Uses combinations of dots (·) and dashes (-) to represent Chinese characters\n• Combines traditional Morse code principles with Chinese language characteristics\n• Provides standardized encoding for Chinese communication"
    },
    {
      "name": "cexp2_title",
      "value": "Characteristics of Encoding Length"
    },
    {
      "name": "cexp2_content",
      "value": "• Chinese Morse code encoding is typically longer than English Morse code\n• This is because Chinese characters are numerous and require more dot-dash combinations\n• Each Chinese character has a unique Morse code representation\n• Increased encoding length ensures character uniqueness"
    },
    {
      "name": "cexp3_title",
      "value": "Historical Development Background"
    },
    {
      "name": "cexp3_content",
      "value": "• 1920s: Initial establishment of Chinese Morse code standards\n• Developed to meet the needs of Chinese telegraph communication\n• International Telecommunication Union (ITU) participated in development\n• Modern standards formed through multiple optimizations"
    },
    {
      "name": "cexp4_title",
      "value": "Application Scenario Analysis"
    },
    {
      "name": "cexp4_content",
      "value": "• Military communication: Secret communication between troops\n• Aviation communication: Communication between pilots and ground control towers\n• Maritime communication: Communication between ships\n• Online chat is not a traditional application scenario"
    },
    {
      "name": "cexp5_title",
      "value": "Learning Strategy Recommendations"
    },
    {
      "name": "cexp5_content",
      "value": "• Starting with basic characters is most scientific\n• Basic characters are used frequently and easy to remember\n• Progressive learning to avoid information overload\n• Build learning confidence and improve efficiency"
    },
