    {
      "name": "telegraph_code_description",
      "value": "Chinese Telegraph Code (also known as \"Chinese Telegraph Cipher\") is a coding system designed for accurate and fast transmission and reception of Chinese characters during the telegraph transmission era.\\n\\n• Origin and Standardization\\nOriginally developed by Sheng Xuanhuai in the late Qing Dynasty, it was later revised multiple times during the Republic of China and New China, forming a unified national standard. The common version is the \"Standard Telegraph Code Book\", which includes more than 7,000 commonly used Chinese characters.\\n\\n• Encoding Method\\nEach Chinese character corresponds to a four-digit numeric code, ranging from 0001 to 9999.\\nFor example:\\n• \"中\" = 0029\\n• \"国\" = 0049\\n• \"电\" = 0168\\n• \"码\" = 0203\\n\\n• Usage\\nIn telegraph transmission, the sender only needs to send the corresponding four-digit number, and the receiver can look up the original Chinese character according to the code book. This ensures accurate transmission and facilitates cross-language communication (numbers are internationally universal).\\n\\nThis page can search by \"Chinese character\" or \"telegraph code\" for quick location."
    },
